%% annotator: Claude (Opus 5), from the article text - NEEDS HUMAN REVIEW
%% provenance: this file REPLACES a map that was byte-identical to
%% setser_may_10_19.mmd and argued the yuan question, which belongs to the
%% 2019-05-10 article ("yuan" appears 17 times there and 0 times here). The
%% yuan argument is unchanged and still lives in setser_may_10_19.mmd. Every
%% node below is drawn from "China Never Stopped Managing its Trade",
%% 2019-05-02, with the source paragraph cited in a trailing comment.
%% See scripts/source_urls.yaml and scripts/README.md Traceability.
flowchart TD

  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["<b>Does a purchase-commitment deal push China back toward managed trade?</b><br/><i>Brad Setser, CFR, May 2019</i>"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["<b>Who buys matters more than the border barrier</b><br/>Tariffs and quotas do not matter much when the bulk of imports are bought by state firms or need a state licence"]
    P2["<b>State control of the commanding heights is not negotiable</b><br/>China will not dismantle the core institutions of its mixed model in a trade negotiation"]
    P3["<b>Managed trade means control of end demand, not tariffs</b><br/>The definitional move the argument turns on - 'that's managed trade in my books, even without formal tariffs or quotas'"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    D1["<b>Canola with Canada shut down</b><br/>Trade stopped without any formal tariff being introduced"]
    D2["<b>Australian coal purchases scaled back</b><br/>No dumping case and no national-security tariff case was filed"]
    D3["<b>U.S. exports went to zero, not down by half</b><br/>A 25% tariff would normally cut imports by something like 50%; several U.S. goods simply stopped"]
    D4["<b>Aircraft: the buyer is the state</b><br/>State-owned airlines plus one state-backed private carrier, with NDRC approval required; China is both Boeing's biggest market and, through COMAC, a future competitor"]
    D5["<b>Soybeans: COFCO controls the infrastructure</b><br/>The old state grain monopoly still controls crushing and distribution, Sinograin steps in from stockpiles, and smaller importers need a state licence"]
    D6["<b>Oil and gas: state majors control the pipes</b><br/>They own the pipelines and the bulk of refining; private LNG terminals exist but the offshore investors are state firms"]
    D7["<b>The capital-goods roll call</b><br/>Telecoms equipment, railway equipment, grid power, avionics - in sector after sector the ultimate buyer is the Chinese state"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["<b>Ownership converts preference into volume</b><br/>When the state controls the importing firms, a few phone calls move trade without any border measure"]
    M2["<b>Border liberalisation cannot create demand</b><br/>GE could own its turbine plant outright and still have to sell to state-run power companies selling to the state grid"]
    M3["<b>Purchasing power is used to force localisation</b><br/>Rolls-Royce would build engines in China to win the contract; Airbus may build the A330 there - so import demand falls as capacity moves in"]
    M4["<b>An administrative lever is reversible by administration</b><br/>Because the control is not legal but ownership-based, what it grants it can also withdraw"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["<b>China never left managed trade</b><br/>So a purchase deal cannot return it to a place it never left"]
    C2["<b>Reform and opening was partial and sector-specific</b><br/>Open to components for re-export and to what China cannot make at home; closed where the state is the buyer"]
    C3["<b>Removing formal barriers is not enough</b><br/>Not even dropping joint-venture requirements changes who the end buyer is"]
    C4["<b>So the standard critique is misdirected</b><br/>The objection is not that the deal reintroduces state involvement, but that it is revocable"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["<b>Main claim: the right critique is revocability, not a return to managed trade</b><br/>China's state never stopped managing a large part of its trade, so the sound objection is Book's and Scissors':<br/>'if it can be negotiated by government fiat, it can be taken away by government fiat'"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Dialectic"]
    X["<b>Consensus view: the deal pushes China back to managed trade</b><br/>Yerxa, Donnan and almost everyone at the IMF Spring meetings - simplistic purchasing commitments reinforce state ownership rather than discourage it"]
    R1["<b>Private-buyer explanation: autos</b><br/>The one sector where the state is not the main buyer. Tariffs were 25% and are still 15%, domestic capacity could meet all demand, yet German marques built a real business - because cars are bought privately"]
    QF["<b>Descriptive caveat</b><br/>This is a description of the world as it is, not as it should be; and medical equipment is a trick case, with private hospitals and local-government procurement"]
    R2["<b>Allies objection, set aside</b><br/>Australia and others argue the United States is selling out its allies for a larger share of a limited import pie"]
  end

